## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of sliding-window streaming algorithms, mergeable sketches, and amortized cost analysis under tight memory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding-window distinct counting
- HyperLogLog mergeability
- Bucketization for windowing
- Memory vs error trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingHLL {
  constructor(K, m) {
    this.hlls = Array.from({length: K}, () => new HyperLogLog(m));
    this.idx = 0;
  }
  update(batch) {
    const i = this.idx % this.hlls.length;
    for (const id of batch) this.hlls[i].add(id);
    this.idx++;
  }
  estimate() {
    const merged = HyperLogLog.merge(this.hlls);
    return merged.estimate();
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked\n\nTests understanding of streaming frequency estimation under tight memory. The MG approach gives a bounded-memory alternative to exact counts, with concrete guarantees and practical trade-offs in real-time systems.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- Streaming algorithms, Misra-Gries, bounded counters, frequency estimation, error bounds, exact vs. approximate modes.\n- Trade-offs: k vs accuracy, batch size B vs throughput.\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
class MGMode {
  constructor(k){
    this.k = k;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.counters.has(x)) this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x)+1);
    else if (this.counters.size < this.k) this.counters.set(x,1);
    else {
      for (const key of Array.from(this.counters.keys()))
        this.counters.set(key, this.counters.get(key)-1);
      for (const [key,c] of Array.from(this.counters))
        if (c<=0) this.counters.delete(key);
    }
  }
  estimate(){
    let best = null, bestc = -1;
    for (const [k,c] of this.counters) if (c>bestc){ bestc=c; best=k; }
    return best;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions\n- How would you adapt to a sliding window instead of a growing stream?\n- How to pick k in practice given M and observed N?\n- What are failure modes if the top-1 item rarely exceeds N/(k+1)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical online data-structure thinking for a sliding-window distinct problem, common in real-time analytics pipelines used by data platforms at Snowflake, Salesforce, and Instacart.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance
- Hash map with counts
- Eviction policy and amortized analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
let window = []; // deque of last W ids
let counts = new Map();
function add(id){
  window.push(id);
  counts.set(id, (counts.get(id) || 0) + 1);
  if (window.length > W){
    const old = window.shift();
    const c = counts.get(old) - 1;
    if (c === 0) counts.delete(old); else counts.set(old,c);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for approximate distinct in extremely high-throughput streams?
- What happens if events arrive in irregular batch sizes or with possible duplicates within a batch?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design efficient dynamic ranking systems with batched updates, requiring you to balance update locality against query efficiency in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Ordered maps for score-based organization
- Lazy maximum score tracking to avoid unnecessary recomputation
- Efficient top-K extraction through bucket traversal
- Amortized time complexity analysis
- Memory usage optimization techniques

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode implementation
class TopKKeeper {
  constructor(users) {
    this.scoreToIds = new TreeMap(); // score -> TreeSet of user IDs
    this.userToScore = new HashMap(); // user ID -> current score
    this.maxScore = null;

    // Initialize in O(n)
    for (const [id, score] of users) {
      this.addUserToBucket(id, score);
    }
  }

  updateBatch(updates) {
    // Process B updates in O(B log n)
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const oldScore = this.userToScore.get(id);
      const newScore = oldScore + delta;

      this.removeUserFromBucket(id, oldScore);
      this.addUserToBucket(id, newScore);
    }
  }

  getTopK(k) {
    // Extract top-K in O(K log n)
    const result = [];
    let currentScore = this.maxScore;
    let remaining = k;

    while (remaining > 0 && currentScore !== null) {
      const ids = this.scoreToIds.get(currentScore);
      const takeCount = Math.min(remaining, ids.size());

      // Extract first 'takeCount' IDs (already sorted)
      result.push(...Array.from(ids).slice(0, takeCount));
      remaining -= takeCount;
      currentScore = this.scoreToIds.lowerKey(currentScore);
    }

    return result.slice(0, k);
  }
}
```

## Optimizations
1. **Lazy Maximum Updates**: Only recompute the maximum score when the current maximum bucket becomes empty, avoiding O(log n) overhead on every update.

2. **Batch Top-K Extraction**: Cache the current top-K result and only recompute when updates affect users in the top-K, reducing query frequency from every batch to only when necessary.

## Comparison to Rebuilding
Rebuilding from scratch would require O(n) time per batch to sort all users, resulting in O(n × batches) total time. Our approach achieves O(B log n) updates and O(K log n) queries, providing significant improvement when K << n and B is moderate.

## Complexity Analysis
- **Initialization**: O(n) - one insertion per user
- **Batch Updates**: O(B log n) - each update requires remove/insert operations
- **Top-K Query**: O(K log n) - traverse at most K score buckets
- **Space**: O(n) - store user-score mappings and score buckets

## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing incremental distance indices for batched graph updates, balancing preprocessing, per-batch work, and constant-time queries. Also probes practical optimizations and comparison against full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental relaxations on edge batches
- Distance labeling with two-way bounds
- Amortized analysis and periodic rebuilds
- Space-efficiency via compact frontiers

## Code Example
```javascript
function batchUpdate(graph, batch, D, distS, distToT) {
  for (const [u,v,w] of batch) {
    if (distS[u] + w + distToT[v] <= D) {
      // relax forward
      if (distS[v] > distS[u] + w) distS[v] = distS[u] + w;
      // backward could be updated similarly if distToT is maintained
    }
  }
}
```

